# Bastille USB lockdown (pre-USBGuard layer)
#
# USBGuard handles the policy-level allow/deny. This rule is a lower-level
# backstop: a USB *device* that is plugged in while the screen is locked
# (swaylock running) is set authorized=0 before a driver binds.
#
# bastille-usb-authorize never touches host controllers / root hubs, and it
# decides "locked" from the swaylock process, not from logind: logind is not
# running yet during boot coldplug, and getting that wrong deauthorizes every
# USB device (keyboard and mouse included) for the whole session.
#
# TO ALLOW A NEW DEVICE
#   Unlock the screen first, then plug in the device.
#   Or: echo 1 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/<bus>-<port>/authorized  (as root)

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", ENV{DEVTYPE}=="usb_device", \
  RUN+="/usr/local/sbin/bastille-usb-authorize %k"
